		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4463&amp;p=34766#p34766"><![CDATA[
<blockquote class="uncited"><div>Dear Roger and all,<br><br>I know that this version is supersed by several ones, but I have a question that probably deals also with all recent versions. It is written :<br><br>• (MD9600 only) Use battery RAM for settings, so that if power is removed from the radio the last change to settings / channel / vfo will be retained<br>• Handle if settings can’t be saved to Flash because of Write failure<br><br>Does it mean that settings ar now only retained thanks to the battery RAM ? And so settings are no more retained in a part of the flash memory ? The battery RAM is not very reliable, specially if the transceiver remains on a shelf for a long time. <br><br>On my side I have kept the last stable version (R20240908), because of my misunderstanding of this new feature.<br><br>Thanks to all the team for the continuous developments, and best wishes for 2025.<br><br>73 - Jean-Roger</div></blockquote>Long story<br><br>Originally, on both the MD9600 and RT3S, we used the battery RAM to store settings, but we found that the RAM battery would go flat after a few weeks of the radio not being used.<br><br>So we changed both the RT3S and MD9600 to use the Flash memory for settings storage.<br><br>This works fine on the RT3S because the settings can be trigged to save to Flash when the radio is turned off by the operator.<br><br>On the MD9600 this was not so satisfactorty, becasue although the settings are saved if the operator presses the power button to turn the radio off.<br><br>However because many people use the MD9600 in their car, connected via the 'ignition' or accessories circuit, which is only powered when the car is running, it was common for the power to be interrupted to the radio when the operator stopped the engine of the car, and the radio was then not saving the data to Flash.<br><br>So I changed the saving system on the MD9600, so that any changes to frequency , channels and options are almost immediatly written to the RAM, so that if the power is interrupted then the last change is not normally lost.<br><br>Also, <span style="text-decoration:underline">when the radio is powered on</span>, the firmware checks the contents of the Flash storage and if it is not up to date, then the settings from RAM are written to Flash<br><br>If the MD9600 is not used for a few weeks, and the internal backup battery goes flat, then when the radio is powered on, the RAM settings will be invalid, and the settings are instead loaded from Flash<br><br><br><br>I hope this explains how the changes to saving, now work on the MD9600<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=50">VK3KYY</a> — Wed Dec 18, 2024 9:20 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
